fr:mainmatter.html:p:
A bicategory \mathcal {K} consists of

A type of objects \mathcal {K}_{0}, or 0-cells

For all x , y : \mathcal {K}_{0}, a category
  \mathcal {\mathcal {K}}_{1}{\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}}. We may elide the subscript and simply
  write this as \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}}. Refer to
  the objects of \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}} as 1-cells between x and
  y, and we may write f : \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}} as
  f : x \to  y or x \overset {f}{\to } y.
  For f , g : \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}}, refer to the morphisms in
  \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}} between f and g as 2-cells and write
  the morphism
  \alpha  : {\mathopen {}\left (\mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}}\right )\mathclose {}} {\mathopen {}\left (f , g\right )\mathclose {}} as
  \alpha  : f \Rightarrow  g or
  f \overset {\alpha }{\Rightarrow } g


For each x : \mathcal {K}_{0}, an identity 1-cell 1_{x} : \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , x\right )\mathclose {}}

For all x , y, z : \mathcal {K}_{0}, a composition functor
     \mathcal {K}_{\star }^{x,y,z} : \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}} \times  \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (y , z\right )\mathclose {}} \to  \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , z\right )\mathclose {}}.
     For 1-cells f : x \to  y and g : y \to  z, write their
     composite as f \star  g : x \to  z

For all w , x, y, z : \mathcal {K}_{0}, a natural isomorphism, the
     associator \alpha  between the two composite functors
     \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (w , x\right )\mathclose {}} \times  \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}} \times  \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (y , z\right )\mathclose {}} \to  \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (w , z\right )\mathclose {}} that
     compose the leftmost, respectively rightmost, pair first:
     \mathcal {K}_{\star }^{w,y,z} \circ  {\mathopen {}\left (\mathcal {K}_{\star }^{w,x,y} \times  \mathrm {id}\right )\mathclose {}}
        \Rightarrow 
        \mathcal {K}_{\star }^{w,x,z} \circ  {\mathopen {}\left (\mathrm {id} \times  \mathcal {K}_{\star }^{x,y,z}\right )\mathclose {}}
     Its component at 1-cells f , g , h is the invertible 2-cell
     \alpha _{f,g,h} : {\mathopen {}\left ({\mathopen {}\left (f \star  g\right )\mathclose {}} \star  h\right )\mathclose {}} \Rightarrow  {\mathopen {}\left (f \star  {\mathopen {}\left (g \star  h\right )\mathclose {}}\right )\mathclose {}}

For all x , y : \mathcal {K}_{0}, natural isomorphisms, the left
     unitor \lambda  and right unitor \rho , each between an
     endofunctor of \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}} and the identity functor:
     \mathcal {K}_{\star }^{x,x,y} \circ  {\mathopen {}\left \langle 1_{x} , \mathrm {id}\right \rangle \mathclose {}} \Rightarrow  \mathrm {id}
        \qquad 
        \mathcal {K}_{\star }^{x,y,y} \circ  {\mathopen {}\left \langle \mathrm {id} , 1_{y}\right \rangle \mathclose {}} \Rightarrow  \mathrm {id}
     where 1_{x} and 1_{y} in the pairings {\mathopen {}\left \langle -,-\right \rangle \mathclose {}} denote
     the constant functors at the identity 1-cells. The components at a
     1-cell f : \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}} are the invertible 2-cells
     \lambda _{f} : 1_{x} \star  f \Rightarrow  f \qquad 
        \rho _{f} : f \star  1_{y} \Rightarrow  f

such that for all f : \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , y\right )\mathclose {}} and g : \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (y , z\right )\mathclose {}} the
     triangle below commutes in \mathcal {K} {\mathopen {}\left (x , z\right )\mathclose {}}:





